    {
      "title": "Postgres 19 repack enables online table bloat cleanup",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://www.snowflake.com/en/blog/engineering/postgres-19-repack-table-bloat/",
      "date": "2026-11-10",
      "authors": [
        "Craig Kerstiens"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how Postgres 19 safely reclaims table bloat online without heavy locks or third-party extensions. You will gain a clear mental model of MVCC space exhaustion and how native concurrent compaction works.",
      "hn_id": "50442488",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50442488",
      "image": "/infographics/02-hn-50371384.jpg",
      "content": "Table bloat is an inevitable consequence of Postgres MVCC architecture, especially on write-heavy workloads. Dead tuples pile up as rows update and delete, leaving behind empty storage that standard autovacuum cannot easily return to the operating system.\n\nHistorically, database administrators faced bad options. Running VACUUM FULL reclaims the space cleanly, but it acquires an exclusive table lock that blocks all reads and writes, bringing production traffic to a standstill. The alternative meant relying on third-party extensions like pg_repack or pg_squeeze, which introduce external dependencies, trigger overhead, and operational risks.\n\nPostgres 19 introduces REPACK CONCURRENTLY directly into core. It rebuilds the bloated relation in the background while the live table remains completely readable and writable throughout the entire rebuild process.\n\nThe command swaps the underlying data pages and re-indexes the table, acquiring only a brief lock at the very end to finish the transition. Running bloat compaction natively without third-party extensions simplifies production maintenance dramatically.\n\nNative concurrent compaction removes one of the most persistent operational pain points in long-running Postgres deployments."
    },
    {
      "title": "Zeroing tiny fractions of weights matches full fine-tuning",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10866",
      "date": "2026-11-10",
      "authors": [
        "Jack Lu",
        "Zhenbang Yang",
        "Mike Lasby",
        "Tejas Pote",
        "Yani Ioannou",
        "Mengye Ren"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how masking less than 0.05% of model weights can match full fine-tuning performance while virtually eliminating catastrophic forgetting. You will learn the geometric mechanisms behind sparse weight deletion across major post-training regimes.",
      "hn_id": "50447363",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50447363",
      "image": "/infographics/02-hn-50447363.jpg",
      "content": "You do not always need to add parameters or perform costly full fine-tuning to teach a large language model new capabilities. Researchers have demonstrated that zeroing out fewer than 0.05 percent of existing weights can match full fine-tuning across reinforcement learning, supervised fine-tuning, and on-policy distillation.\n\nDubbed Deletion Tuning, the approach optimizes a binary mask exclusively over the gate projection in transformer blocks. By analyzing weight geometry through stable rank and spectrum drift, the authors found that masking alone mirrors the exact trajectory of full fine-tuning far better than LoRA or random sparse pruning.\n\nThe real operational win lies in continual learning and deployment footprint. The learned binary mask is orders of magnitude smaller than a standard checkpoint. More importantly, while full fine-tuning and LoRA suffer up to a 5.4 point drop on prior tasks under reinforcement learning, masking keeps base accuracy intact.\n\nPost-training efficiency might not require complex parameter additions when selective pruning can alter model behavior cleanly."
    },
    {
      "title": "Achieving kernel memory safety by compiling ownership to C11",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://ceesafe.github.io/CeeSafe/blog/kernel-safety-through-c",
      "date": "2026-11-10",
      "authors": [
        "vkuttyp"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how CeeSafe delivers Rust-style memory safety to kernels without the complexity of a second compiler toolchain. You will learn the tradeoffs of transpiling ownership semantics into native C11.",
      "hn_id": "50444873",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50444873",
      "image": "/infographics/03-hn-50444873.jpg",
      "content": "Introducing Rust into existing C kernel codebases often introduces major friction because it requires a dual-compiler toolchain. Teams must pin both GCC and rustc via LLVM, generate bindings across headers with libclang, and synchronize target code-generation flags across different compiler backends.\n\nCeeSafe offers an alternative architectural trade-off. Instead of compiling directly to machine code through LLVM, its compiler emits warning-clean freestanding C11 code. The kernel then compiles that output directly using its existing C compiler, headers, and CFLAGS.\n\nUnder the hood, CeeSafe enforces the standard Rust safety model: single ownership, move semantics, shared and exclusive borrows, and deterministic destruction without a garbage collector. The kernel build receives relocatable objects without forcing downstream infrastructure to maintain two parallel compilation stacks.\n\nCompiling memory-safe semantics down to standard C represents a compelling middle ground for legacy infrastructure that cannot absorb a second systems toolchain."
    },
    {
      "title": "Agentic AI formally verifies the Signal cryptographic protocol",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://deepmind.google/blog/introducing-alphaproof-code/",
      "date": "2026-11-10",
      "authors": [
        "Pushmeet Kohli",
        "Swarat Chaudhuri"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how multi-agent AI systems can formally verify complex, real-world cryptographic code. You will learn how DeepMind paired human domain experts with automated theorem proving to mathematically verify Signal.",
      "hn_id": "50439193",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50439193",
      "image": "/infographics/04-hn-50439193.jpg",
      "content": "Google DeepMind has demonstrated the first automated formal verification of a production cryptographic protocol at scale. Using AlphaProof Code, an agentic multi-LLM framework powered by Gemini, researchers verified the actual code implementing the Signal protocol.\n\nFormal verification remains the gold standard for software security, yet it has historically been too labor-intensive for massive codebases. AlphaProof Code addresses this by splitting the verification pipeline much like a human research laboratory. Human cryptographers establish formal mathematical specifications, while specialized agent swarms synthesize over six million lines of Lean code to mechanically prove those correctness properties.\n\nThis marks a significant milestone in automated reasoning. Moving from high-level synthesis to machine-checked mathematical proofs transforms LLMs from fallible assistants into rigorous verification engines for mission-critical distributed systems and security protocols.\n\nAgentic frameworks succeed most when bound to deterministic, machine-checked feedback loops."
    },
    {
      "title": "Single tar archive extracts eight distinct ways across tools",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://stoppels.ch/2026/11/07/the-hardest-tarball.html",
      "date": "2026-11-10",
      "authors": [
        "stabbles"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how subtle ambiguities in the POSIX tar specification allow a single archive to produce completely different extraction results across major tools.",
      "hn_id": "50441338",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50441338",
      "image": "/infographics/05-hn-50441338.jpg",
      "content": "File format specifications frequently contain subtle ambiguities that modern tools resolve in completely contradictory ways. A single GNU tar command can craft an archive that unpacks into eight different layouts depending strictly on whether you use GNU tar, bsdtar, Go, Python, or BusyBox.\n\nThe trick relies on setting a pax size header of exactly 512 bytes on a hard link entry. Under POSIX specifications, hard links may optionally include data blocks, but the standard does not clearly define how parsers must handle unexpected payloads. While GNU tar and Go ignore the size header and extract zero bytes, bsdtar treats the subsequent 512 bytes as hard link content, consuming the metadata block of the file that immediately follows.\n\nEvery backend engineer who ingests untrusted archives in build pipelines or container runtimes should inspect these divergence patterns. Relying on default tar implementations without canonical normalization creates severe security desynchronization risks across decoupled services.\n\nNever assume that a standardized archive format means standard extraction behavior across your systems."
    },
    {
      "title": "What objective is reasoning solving",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://alexwang.ai/posts/reasoning-as-pathfinding/",
      "date": "2026-11-10",
      "authors": [
        "Alex Wang"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to explore the underlying mathematical objectives and formal definitions that drive reasoning in machine learning models.",
      "hn_id": "50445013",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50445013",
      "image": "/infographics/06-hn-50445013.jpg",
      "content": "Most explanations of LLM reasoning treat chain-of-thought as an emergent behavioral quirk rather than a well-defined mathematical problem. By framing multi-step reasoning as an explicit graph pathfinding objective, we can finally analyze how models navigate intermediate states to arrive at correct conclusions.\n\nTraditional next-token prediction minimizes cross-entropy across individual transitions, but true reasoning requires finding valid trajectories through latent search spaces. When you formalize this problem as pathfinding over token graphs, several trade-offs between heuristic search, rollout depth, and value estimation become immediately apparent.\n\nUnderstanding these formal properties allows backend teams to design more effective reasoning harnesses and tree-search decoders. The underlying mechanics show that better reasoning is not merely a byproduct of higher parameter counts, but of how effectively an inference framework explores and prunes prospective trajectories."
    },
    {
      "title": "Open models make self-hosted inference practical for platform teams",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://docs.modelplane.ai/guides/inference-guide",
      "date": "2026-11-10",
      "authors": [
        "d-ramdass"
      ],
      "why_read": "This guide explains why organizations are shifting to self-hosted open models and how platform engineers can run production inference. Readers will learn how to cut costs, control latency, and manage the infrastructure required for AI workloads.",
      "hn_id": "50443714",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50443714",
      "image": "/infographics/07-hn-50443714.jpg",
      "content": "Serving open-weights models in production changes platform architecture from top to bottom. Most teams start by wrapping a model in an HTTP endpoint, only to hit punishing memory ceilings and unpredictable tail latencies once traffic scales.\n\nRunning large language model inference efficiently requires understanding how model weights map to hardware memory bandwidth. Batching techniques, memory fragmentation in the key-value cache, and GPU tensor parallelism determine whether an inference cluster runs at ten percent or seventy percent hardware utilization. Moving from managed APIs to self-hosted instances can reduce infrastructure spend by up to eighty percent at scale, but that margin evaporates quickly without proper attention to memory layout and execution runtimes.\n\nTreating models as specialized, memory-bound workloads rather than generic microservices is the key to stable and economical inference systems."
    },
    {
      "title": "Real-world browser fingerprinting mitigations reduce user identifiability significantly",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://fingerprinting.study/",
      "date": "2026-11-10",
      "authors": [
        "Tom Ritter",
        "Fatih Kilic",
        "Frederik Braun",
        "Elisa Luo",
        "Geoffrey M. Voelker",
        "Stefan Savage",
        "Christoph Kerschbaumer"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how large-scale empirical measurement can identify browser uniqueness factors and evaluate real-world defenses without breaking web compatibility.",
      "hn_id": "50444684",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50444684",
      "image": "/infographics/08-hn-50444684.jpg",
      "content": "Tracking users across the web via browser fingerprinting has long been treated as an intractable privacy problem. A recent large-scale measurement study across 25 million Firefox users reveals the actual statistical surface area of fingerprinting in production environments.\n\nBy evaluating uniqueness across millions of unbiased real-world endpoints using Oblivious HTTP (OHTTP), researchers isolated the specific system metrics that leak the highest entropy. They found that fine-grained hardware and rendering characteristics allow commercial tracking scripts to isolate 75 percent of standard browser profiles.\n\nTo counter this without breaking web compatibility, the engineering team introduced systematic metric normalization. These defensive countermeasures successfully lowered the proportion of uniquely identifiable clients from 75 percent down to 46 percent, proving that deliberate entropy reduction can curtail passive identification at scale."
    },
    {
      "title": "Overcoming hardware memory walls in edge model inference",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://neuracryption.com/research",
      "date": "2026-11-10",
      "authors": [
        "NeuraCryption Research"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand the physical hardware bottlenecks throttling on-device language models and how channel tensor reconstruction bypasses memory bandwidth limits.",
      "hn_id": "50440359",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50440359",
      "image": "/infographics/09-hn-50440359.jpg",
      "content": "Autoregressive LLM inference does not bottleneck on arithmetic compute density; it suffocates on memory bandwidth.\n\nOn a consumer GPU with 192 GB/s memory bandwidth, executing a 7-billion parameter FP16 model means transferring 14 gigabytes across the memory bus for every generated token. Even if your tensor cores can compute thirty trillion operations per second, arithmetic intensity during single-batch decoding remains close to one operation per byte transferred. When weights do not fit entirely inside VRAM, PCIe bus transfers kill generation speed completely.\n\nThe SPECTRA architecture tackles this physical limitation through sparse-precision channel tensor reconstruction. Instead of performing standard post-training quantization that often damages critical outliers or demands expensive calibration loops, it uses direct AST normalization scales to compress models by 3.3x with virtually zero memory leaks.\n\nRunning DeepSeek-R1 down from 3.31 GB to 1.03 GB allows sustained 82 tokens per second on modest 4 GB GPUs without host paging. Hardware-conscious architecture beats throwing more raw compute at bad memory layouts."
    },
    {
      "title": "Targeted lateral exploits breach customer variables in Railway incident",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://blog.railway.com/p/incident-report-october-security-incident",
      "date": "2026-11-10",
      "authors": [
        "Jacob Cooper",
        "Angelo Saraceno",
        "Sarah Bedell",
        "Luca Casonato"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this postmortem to understand how an automated threat actor chained exploits to target AI provider credentials across cloud infrastructure. You will gain insight into the mechanics of high-speed defensive containment against parallelized attacks.",
      "hn_id": "50441465",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50441465",
      "image": "/infographics/10-hn-50441465.jpg",
      "content": "Security incident response is transforming into an automated race between defenders and autonomous offensive agents.\n\nIn Railway's recent security post-mortem, attackers breached infrastructure and moved laterally across clusters to compromise customer environment variables. What makes this attack distinct from conventional intrusion patterns is the methodology: the adversary deployed autonomous harnesses powered by uncensored models to probe, chain exploits, and adapt in real time.\n\nDefenders were not merely contesting a human hacker working through scripts. They were fighting an automated feedback loop executing parallel exploit paths at machine speed, specifically hunting for AI provider API tokens intended for model distillation pipelines.\n\nRailway halted builds twice, rotated tokens across the board, and purged all active user sessions to sever lateral movement paths. As attackers weaponize autonomous agent harnesses, manual runbooks will no longer suffice; defense must rely on automated policy verification and continuous secret compartmentalization."
    },
    {
      "title": "Ceph rewritten in Rust delivers wire-compatible flash performance",
      "source": "github",
      "url": "https://github.com/croit/ceph-rust",
      "date": "2026-11-10",
      "authors": [
        "croit"
      ],
      "why_read": "Learn how a ground-up Rust rewrite of Ceph achieves high-throughput flash storage and seamless drop-in compatibility with existing clusters.",
      "hn_id": "50437226",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50437226",
      "image": "/infographics/11-github-50437226.jpg",
      "content": "Classic distributed storage architectures often hit severe scalability walls when ported to modern NVMe drives. Ceph was originally built around HDD-era storage assumptions, meaning lock contention and deep abstraction layers quickly saturate CPU cores long before exhausting disk bus bandwidth.\n\nFlashStore approaches this problem with a clean slate by rewriting Ceph OSD in Rust. It enforces a thread-per-core model, lock-free hot execution paths, zero-copy data pipelines, and direct integration with RDMA and GPUDirect. Most importantly, it remains strictly wire-compatible with the upstream Ceph protocol, allowing single-node drop-in replacements without requiring a forklift migration of monitors or client libraries.\n\nDesigning around hardware constraints rather than retrofitting legacy concurrency primitives remains the best way to extract true gigabyte-per-second performance from modern solid-state storage."
    },
    {
      "title": "Webcam person detection without ever exposing video frames",
      "source": "github",
      "url": "https://github.com/belfortlabs/blindsight-demo",
      "date": "2026-11-10",
      "authors": [
        "sambejk"
      ],
      "why_read": "Learn how homomorphic encryption enables real-time visual inference without exposing raw camera frames to a remote server. It demonstrates a practical end-to-end architecture for privacy-preserving machine learning on edge devices.",
      "hn_id": "50437839",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50437839",
      "image": "/infographics/12-github-50437839.jpg",
      "content": "Running machine learning inference on sensitive user data usually forces a tough architectural compromise: either trust remote servers with unencrypted raw data, or run models locally under tight client compute constraints. Fully homomorphic encryption (FHE) eliminates this dilemma by evaluating neural networks directly on encrypted ciphertexts.\n\nBlindSight demonstrates this in practice by performing real-time person detection on webcam streams without ever exposing unencrypted frames to the server. The client captures frames, transforms them into model input dimensions, encrypts the arrays using client-held keys, and ships ciphertexts to the inference engine. The backend processes the model directly on encrypted values and returns encrypted scores, completing the entire round trip in under one second.\n\nFor systems engineers designing privacy-first pipelines, decoupling inference compute from plain-text data access removes massive compliance and security hurdles. This project offers a concrete blueprint for production-grade zero-trust model hosting.\n\nEvaluating neural networks directly in ciphertext space is finally moving from academic curiosity to practical software architecture."
    },
    {
      "title": "Unsafe code in Rust standard library triggers undefined behavior",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://forge-blog.github.io/posts/rust-std-memory-safety/",
      "date": "2026-11-10",
      "authors": [
        "matt_d"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how hidden flaws in Rust's standard library can break memory-safety guarantees and trigger undefined behavior in safe code.",
      "hn_id": "50443240",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50443240",
      "image": "/infographics/13-hn-50443240.jpg",
      "content": "Safe Rust promises to eliminate undefined behavior, but that guarantee rests entirely on the correctness of the unsafe code underpinning foundational libraries.\n\nRecent analysis from Microsoft FORGE Lab revealed multiple soundness bugs in Rust core and standard libraries where purely safe application code could trigger memory corruption. The researchers isolated three recurring patterns of misplaced trust in unsafe blocks: assuming foreign code will never panic, assuming user implementations of traits honor documented contracts, and assuming external system environments behave consistently.\n\nWhen an unsafe block performs pointer arithmetic based on intermediate collection lengths and an unexpected panic unwinds through that frame, drop implementations can encounter uninitialized memory or cause double frees. Safe Rust cannot prevent these errors if the internal unsafe abstraction fails to protect against non-deterministic state changes.\n\nWriting sound unsafe Rust requires treating safe external inputs and unwinding paths with the same paranoia typically reserved for untrusted network input."
    },
    {
      "title": "Running Zanzibar authorization in-process enables microsecond permission checks",
      "source": "github",
      "url": "https://github.com/Pragadeesh-19/nodusdb",
      "date": "2026-11-10",
      "authors": [
        "Pragadeesh-19"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how embedding Zanzibar authorization directly into an application process eliminates network latency for microsecond checks. You will learn how pairing an in-memory graph with an Iceberg-backed signed log provides auditability and durability without dedicated servers.",
      "hn_id": "50441801",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50441801",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Calling an external authorization service like SpiceDB or OpenFGA over the network introduces a baseline latency penalty of 3 to 5 milliseconds per check. When complex API handlers require multiple recursive permission evaluations, that network overhead quickly dominates your response time.\n\nNodusDB takes the embedded route by keeping the relationship graph inside process memory. A fresh check takes roughly 14 microseconds at the median across more than 500,000 relationships, completely eliminating RPC bottlenecks for high-throughput services.\n\nDurability and replication are handled through a signed, append-only log written directly to object storage. Because that log is structured as an Apache Iceberg table, you get full SQL audit capabilities, time travel, and compliance erasure out of the box without operating a dedicated database cluster.\n\nEmbedding authorization graphs in-process is a compelling pattern for low-latency backend architectures."
    },
    {
      "title": "Git fixes critical code execution and path traversal vulnerabilities",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://github.blog/open-source/git/git-security-vulnerabilities-announced-7/",
      "date": "2026-11-10",
      "authors": [
        "Elijah Newren"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how integer truncation and unvalidated symrefs enable arbitrary code execution in Git during clones, and learn the remediation steps required.",
      "hn_id": "50443941",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50443941",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Git 2.56.1 patches six severe vulnerabilities, highlighted by a remote code execution exploit triggered simply by running a recursive clone on an untrusted repository. The mechanics behind CVE-2026-111760 show why low-level integer handling remains one of the trickiest areas in systems engineering.\n\nWhile Git enforces strict path validation to prevent repositories from overwriting their internal metadata, an integer truncation bug in index parsing allowed crafted, extremely long paths to pass validation under one length and then get written under a shorter truncated name. An attacker can exploit this mismatch during recursive submodule clones to swap a submodule's .git file with a symbolic link pointing to malicious hooks, achieving arbitrary execution.\n\nAnother flaw, CVE-2026-113408, allowed path traversal via a server-advertised HEAD symref targeting unborn branches in protocol v2. If your automated CI/CD runners or local development environments pull external submodules without explicit sandboxing, these vulnerabilities pose a direct operational threat.\n\nAudit your pipeline infrastructure and patch your build workers to the maintenance releases immediately."
    },
    {
      "title": "Designing thread interfaces as collaborative document editors",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://delta.dev/blog/building-a-thread-ui-on-hard-mode",
      "date": "2026-11-10",
      "authors": [
        "Agus Zubiaga"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand why standard chat interfaces and traditional code editor indexing break down for interactive AI agent threads. It breaks down how to rethink editor architecture from first principles by treating conversation history as a unified document.",
      "hn_id": "50445967",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50445967",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Most AI agent interfaces rely on chat bubbles, but building an interactive canvas where users and autonomous agents edit code, terminals, and prompts simultaneously breaks traditional editor architectures. The team behind Delta started by adapting Zed's high-performance editor model, but quickly hit a wall.\n\nZed relies on a hierarchy of indexes that incrementally map the text buffer to screen coordinates. That works when drawing plain code, but modern agent interactions contain dynamic, stateful elements like live-streaming terminal blocks, foldable cards, and inline tool executions. Because these widgets constantly mutate their visual bounds without altering the underlying text buffer, the layout indexes continually drifted out of sync.\n\nRather than patching incremental state machines, they redesigned the editor from scratch. They backed the unified thread with DeltaDB, a text CRDT powered by a Rope, turning the entire conversation into a shared, collaborative document where human edits and agent actions resolve deterministically.\n\nTreating agent interactions as structured document sync rather than ephemeral chat streams fundamentally stabilizes complex human-agent workflows."
    },
    {
      "title": "Jsonb storage overhead depends on document whitespace and compression",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://now-next.nl/en/insights/postgresql-json-vs-jsonb-measured/",
      "date": "2026-11-10",
      "authors": [
        "millspore"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how document structure, formatting whitespace, and compression algorithms impact storage size and query speed differences between json and jsonb in PostgreSQL. You will learn empirical benchmarks to make informed architectural decisions for semi-structured data.",
      "hn_id": "50437540",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50437540",
      "image": null,
      "content": "PostgreSQL documentation often advises engineers to prefer jsonb over plain json, but the actual trade-offs depend heavily on your payload and compression settings.\n\nRecent benchmarks on PostgreSQL 17 and 18 reveal that storing compact, two-hundred-byte documents can consume almost eighteen percent more disk space when using jsonb instead of json. However, if your application stores formatted documents with whitespace, jsonb actually saves space because it normalizes the structure and eliminates redundant formatting. TOAST compression through LZ4 or PGLZ alters this equation further once document sizes cross storage thresholds.\n\nOn the query side, jsonb delivers unequivocal performance advantages. Extracting a single top-level key using the arrow operator is nearly eleven times faster than scanning raw json text. Plain json must parse the raw string on every read, whereas jsonb maintains an internal binary representation optimized for direct key lookups, containment checks, and indexing.\n\nStorage costs might occasionally favor plain json for raw log archives, but jsonb remains essential for active query paths."
    },
    {
