      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how Mantis translates web pages into concise numbered text instead of screenshots. You will learn how structured text representations cut token usage and latency for autonomous agents.",
      "hn_id": "50441765",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50441765",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Most browser-use agent frameworks waste massive token budgets by feeding full DOM trees or high-resolution screenshots into LLM contexts. Mantis takes a completely different path by serializing webpage state into compact, numbered textual representations designed specifically for tool-calling agents.\n\nIn head-to-head testing against standard accessibility-tree implementations like Playwright MCP, this approach achieved a 3.5x to 5x reduction in input tokens while cutting end-to-end task latency roughly in half. It also dropped required model invocations from nearly thirty calls down to around a dozen for long web navigation flows.\n\nThe critical takeaway for systems engineers is that providing raw unstructured structural trees often degrades agent performance. Sanitizing and pre-indexing actionable interactive elements upstream yields both faster execution and significantly higher task completion rates."
    },
    {
      "title": "Dropping IPv4 options fails to fully mitigate CVE-2026-98432",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://www.firezone.dev/blog/linux-tcp-flaw",
      "date": "2026-11-10",
      "authors": [
        "jamilbk"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand why partial workarounds for CVE-2026-98432 fail and how the Linux TCP vulnerability operates. You will learn why a kernel upgrade is the only complete fix.",
      "hn_id": "50440821",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50440821",
      "image": null,
      "content": "A critical use-after-free vulnerability in the Linux kernel TCP stack demonstrates why naive network mitigation strategies frequently fail in production infrastructure. While initial advisories recommended dropping incoming IPv4 packets containing IP options at the edge router, that workaround leaves substantial attack surface exposed.\n\nTraffic traversing encapsulated tunnels, local loopback interfaces, and container network namespaces can still deliver triggering packets directly into vulnerable kernel handlers. Furthermore, popular belief that disabling Multipath TCP eliminates the attack vector is incorrect because the issue resides in core TCP connection handshake handling rather than MPTCP extensions.\n\nEngineers operating exposed backend services must realize that protocol-level edge filters rarely replace core kernel patching. When state machines inside the transport layer break, packet filtering rules cannot reliably model every internal socket transition."
    },
    {
      "title": "Switching LLMs mid-conversation is a ski-rental problem",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://www.edgee.ai/blog/posts/switching-llms-mid-conversation-is-a-ski-rental-problem",
      "date": "2026-11-10",
      "authors": [
        "Khaled Maâmra"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand why dynamically switching models during long multi-turn conversations requires balancing cache rebuild overhead against inference savings. It frames the caching tradeoff as an online ski-rental problem with practical routing strategies.",
      "hn_id": "50442877",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50442877",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Most LLM routing strategies evaluate requests in isolation: a classifier routes simple queries to small models and complex queries to frontier models. In multi-turn agentic workflows, this naive routing breaks down because it ignores prompt caching.\n\nEvery time a router switches models mid-session, the downstream provider cannot reuse the established prefix cache. Rebuilding that context history on a new model incurs an immediate latency and token penalty that often wipes out the cost savings of using a cheaper model.\n\nTreating model switching as an online ski-rental problem provides a formal mathematical framework for this trade-off. Instead of switching immediately when task difficulty drops, the router waits until the cumulative savings of the cheaper model exceed the one-time switching cost of cache invalidation.\n\nEvaluating routing policies against historical trace schedules allows teams to optimize both latency and infrastructure spend without thrashing model context."
    },
    {
      "title": "Durable execution helps long-horizon agents survive real-world failures",
      "source": "hn",
