      "date": "2026-11-10",
      "authors": [
        "Kiran Prakash"
      ],
      "why_read": "Learn how durable execution provides a reliable programming model for long-running AI agents to recover from process crashes and external failures.",
      "hn_id": "50440853",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50440853",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Autonomous agents working on complex, multi-day tasks inevitably run into process crashes, network timeouts, and external state mutations. If an agent loops over LLM calls without deterministic persistence, any unhandled failure forces an expensive restart from scratch.\n\nApplying durable execution paradigms solves this reliability gap. By recording the outcome of every interaction with external tools, APIs, and humans, the runtime can rebuild the agent state machine deterministically. When an unexpected crash occurs, previously completed steps are skipped rather than re-executed.\n\nHowever, durability alone does not guarantee semantic correctness. Long-horizon workflows introduce temporal drift where the external environment changes while the agent waits for human input, requiring distinct handling for idempotency, compensation, and state validation.\n\nDecoupling prompt reasoning from the underlying durable workflow runtime is essential for running reliable agentic automation in production."
    },
