      "content": "Pretraining massive mixture-of-experts models demands careful optimization trade-offs that go far beyond standard transformer layers. The newly completed 18T-token run of Marin 535B-A23B demonstrates several architectural innovations designed to maximize training stability and throughput.\n\nThe model utilizes a shallow architecture with 48 layers and a 6144 hidden dimension, paired with a 3-to-1 ratio of local sliding-window to global attention. By routing across 384 latent experts with 2 shared experts and employing short convolutions (SConv) with a kernel size of 4 across key projections and MLP outputs, the design significantly stabilizes token routing.\n\nEqually notable is the optimizer choice: rather than standard distributed AdamW, the training run relied on Hyperball and Muon variants across all weight matrices, paired with top-k quantile balancing to regulate logit drift.\n\nFor engineers scaling frontier-scale transformer workloads, these concrete layer choices and routing dynamics provide an empirical blueprint for large-scale MoE runs."
