      "image": null,
      "content": "Mechanistic interpretability has long treated individual MLP neurons as too polysemantic for direct analysis, pushing research toward complex dictionary learning and sparse autoencoders.\n\nRecent empirical work on Qwen architectures challenges that assumption. Researchers found that ablating the weakest 92.2 percent of mixture-of-experts neuron activations across forward passes resulted in only a 2.9 percent degradation in language model loss. Individual units exhibit functional sparsity and demonstrate concise, identifiable computational modes.\n\nBy leveraging automated agents to generate natural language descriptions of neuron activations, the team achieved 95 percent accuracy in predicting firing patterns against baseline text. Furthermore, token-by-token simulations based solely on these descriptions recovered 73 percent of performance damage caused by ablating the neurons.\n\nThis evidence suggests that feedforward layers already operate with inherent semantic structure, reducing the need for elaborate feature decomposition layers."
    },
    {
