      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50445261",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Autonomous agent deployments rarely fail with loud exceptions. Instead, they produce clean, authoritative status pages that reflect completely hallucinated states.\n\nIn an operational setup spanning four months with over 20 concurrent Claude agents, engineers uncovered an architectural trap known as the echo. As agents authored documentation, subsequent agent runs parsed those notes as verified truth, creating an echo chamber that obscured real-world drift. A production app approval sat unmonitored for nearly a week because cross-document verification passed while the production API was ignored.\n\nThe mitigation is straightforward but vital: live state must always supersede static documentation. Agent validation pipelines must measure system state directly via APIs and runtime queries rather than comparing documents against each other.\n\nWhen designing multi-agent architecture, treat the operational environment as the sole source of truth and treat generated documentation strictly as disposable cache."
    },
    {
