      "content": "Parameter counts are a misleading proxy when optimizing large language models for production throughput and latency. Memory bandwidth, kernel launch overheads, and serving-engine batch schedules dictate runtime performance far more than raw parameter totals.\n\nAutoCompress addresses this disconnect by integrating serving-engine hardware cost models directly into differentiable architecture search. Instead of uniform pruning, it jointly calculates global and per-layer structural modifications validated against real hardware measurements. When applied to Nemotron 3.5 Super, it compressed the network from 120 billion down to 74 billion parameters, preserving 95 percent of baseline benchmark accuracy while enabling single-GPU BF16 serving at a 128k context window.\n\nTreating the deployment hardware and the model graph as a coupled optimization problem yields significantly better latency profiles than traditional heuristic pruning.\n\nEngineers must design compression strategies around memory bus limits rather than theoretical parameter counts."
    },
    {
      "title": "Benchmarking language models on long-horizon harness engineering",
