      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50445107",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Most agent evaluations measure downstream code generation while keeping the execution harness static. This obscures the critical role of scaffolding: the sandbox containment, context parsing, and environment feedback loops that determine whether an agent succeeds or fails.\n\nDoc2Harness evaluates this dynamic in reverse by benchmarking whether frontier models can construct complete, isolated agent harnesses from scratch. The harness must implement interface contracts derived from production agent runtimes, complete with environment coordination and tool bridges. The constructed environments are subsequently verified using isolated containers where mock models stress test the file system operations, process lifecycle controls, and CLI streams across 328 test scenarios.\n\nThe benchmark demonstrates wide variance between frontier systems: top models attained a 53 percent build success rate, while baseline frontier models scored as low as 27 percent. Failures consistently occurred around edge cases in process termination signals and error serialization.\n\nEngineers building autonomous coding platforms should focus as much rigor on harness architecture as on model selection."
    },
    {
      "title": "Benchmarking multimodal agents across visually complex real-world tasks",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/models-and-research/gemini-models/multimodal-knowledge-agent-benchmark/",
      "date": "2026-11-10",
      "authors": [
        "Rohan Doshi",
        "Ben Caine"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how the MKA benchmark evaluates AI agents on complex, visually grounded professional tasks rather than simple text-only prompts. You will learn the methodology behind measuring multi-step domain-specific reasoning.",
      "hn_id": "50442367",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50442367",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Most existing agent benchmarks test purely text-based workflows that fail to reflect actual engineering and domain tasks. In real work, problems require cross-referencing intricate CAD blueprints, circuit schematics, multi-sheet financial models, and sprawling reports. Measuring model performance on simple text prompts does not tell you if an agent can execute end-to-end tasks in production.\n\nGoogle DeepMind and Artificial Analysis introduced the Multimodal Knowledge Agent (MKA) benchmark to close this gap. Spanning 83 occupations across 10 industries, the benchmark evaluates agents on 200 tasks that each demand three to eight hours of human work. Models must consume heterogeneous multimodal documents and satisfy an average of 70 objective criteria per task, with grading weighted 75 percent toward factual accuracy.\n\nThis benchmark demonstrates that agent failure modes rarely stem from basic natural language understanding. Instead, failures emerge from poor visual spatial reasoning, inability to maintain context across multi-page document sets, and hallucinated calculations during multi-step inference chains.\n\nEvaluating agents on realistic, artifact-heavy tasks is the only way to expose how brittle current reasoning architectures remain."
    },
    {
      "title": "Orthogonal obfuscation protects vector databases without sacrificing similarity search",
      "source": "github",
      "url": "https://github.com/cernica/hadamask",
      "date": "2026-11-10",
      "authors": [
        "cernica"
      ],
      "why_read": "Learn how client-side orthogonal transformations can protect sensitive embedding vectors against database breaches while preserving exact cosine and distance calculations.",
      "hn_id": "50441783",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50441783",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Storing raw vector embeddings alongside plaintext metadata in managed databases exposes sensitive organizational knowledge if a snapshot or read credential leaks. Traditional database-level encryption shields data at rest, but vector engines still require raw floats to calculate distances during similarity search.\n\nHadamask offers an elegant mathematical workaround by applying a secret keyed orthogonal transformation to vectors on the client before they ever leave your application. Because orthogonal matrices inherently preserve inner products and Euclidean norms, standard cosine, dot product, and L2 distance metrics remain completely identical to operations run over raw vectors. Your index layout, recall rate, and database engine work out of the box without custom distance operators.\n\nSimultaneously, the library encrypts surrounding metadata fields using AES-256-GCM with distinct nonces and length padding. This ensures that an unauthorized snapshot dump yields neither meaningful semantic vector embeddings nor plaintext payload details.\n\nFor teams deploying enterprise RAG pipelines into third-party cloud vector stores, client-side linear transformations offer a clean, robust pattern for zero-trust vector security."
    },
    {
