      "image": null,
      "content": "Benchmarking autonomous AI agents has become notoriously untrustworthy. In a thorough audit of Terminal Bench 4.1, researchers examined all 66 agent evaluation tasks and discovered that 23 of them suffer from severe grading flaws, test leakage, or environment setup bugs.\n\nThe core issue lies within the verifier architecture. In many benchmark harnesses, tests run in mutable shared environments where agent execution can inadvertently alter test fixtures or bypass checks entirely. Even when benchmarks attempt process isolation using worker containers, verifier scripts often fail to account for valid alternative implementations, grading working algorithmic optimizations as failures.\n\nEvaluating terminal agents requires defensive engineering. Verifiers must run in isolated sandboxes with strictly separated execution boundaries, static test definitions, and deterministic system states. If your evaluation suite is flawed, optimizing your model harness is merely chasing noise."
    },
    {
