      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand the concrete memory-latency tradeoff when retiring idle database connections. You will learn how reducing pool sizes cuts PostgreSQL memory usage at the expense of higher connection acquisition latency during traffic spikes.",
      "hn_id": "50446854",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50446854",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Default connection pool configurations quietly waste huge amounts of database memory across distributed microservice fleets. When dozens of application instances maintain fixed-size connection pools, PostgreSQL must maintain hundreds of idle backend worker processes, each consuming dedicated memory.\n\nA reproducible benchmark using Spring Boot, HikariCP, and PostgreSQL 16 quantifies this operational tradeoff. Allowing twenty idle connections to scale down to two released twenty-nine megabytes of cgroup memory on a single quiet instance. In an architecture with thirty instances maintaining thirty-two connections, that idle baseline easily accumulates hundreds of unnecessary database backends.\n\nThe penalty appears during burst recovery. When incoming traffic suddenly demands new connections after a lull, median connection acquisition latency jumps from under three milliseconds to roughly two hundred milliseconds as TCP handshakes and PostgreSQL worker processes initialize.\n\nDeciding to retire idle connections requires balancing database host memory headroom against tolerance for cold connection latency spikes."
    },
