        "Hanna Marszałek",
        "Jakub Janowski"
      ],
      "why_read": "Discover how to bridge Rust and C# async runtimes over a C ABI to build unified database drivers. You will learn the limitations of existing bindgen tools and the mechanics of async FFI design.",
      "hn_id": "50444437",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50444437",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Bridging asynchronous runtimes across foreign function boundaries is notoriously tricky. When ScyllaDB decided to consolidate their database drivers around a core Rust implementation, they ran into a fundamental barrier: C# has no official tooling to wire the Tokio event loop into the .NET TaskCompletionSource model over a C ABI.\n\nInstead of blocking threads or spawning unbounded native workers, the engineering team built an async FFI bridge. The system leverages Rust handles passed across the C ABI that notify the .NET thread pool when a native Future resolves. This allows .NET to await driver operations natively without burning operating system threads on synchronization stalls.\n\nUnifying database drivers down to a single Rust engine simplifies shard-aware routing and topology tracking across language ecosystems. If you build cross-language tooling or high-throughput clients, inspecting how they handle memory ownership and async task waking is well worth your time.\n\nSmart runtime interop saves teams from rewriting complex distributed protocols in multiple programming languages."
    },
    {
      "title": "Compiling agent experience into persistent knowledge enables skill evolution",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.27454",
      "date": "2026-11-10",
      "authors": [
        "Liyan Tang",
        "Cyrus Rashtchian",
        "Chun-Sung Ferng",
        "Andrew Tomkins",
        "Da-Cheng Juan",
        "Tu Vu"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how systematically consolidating agent experience into a persistent knowledge base drives continuous skill evolution. You will learn how separating knowledge accumulation from execution helps smaller models outperform larger baselines and enables effective cross-model skill transfer.",
      "hn_id": "50441125",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50441125",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Most autonomous agent frameworks discard execution traces after a run or dump raw histories into an unbounded context window. Over extended sessions, this strategy leads to severe context drift and degrades reasoning capability.\n\nThe WikiSkill framework addresses this breakdown by decoupling execution traces, accumulated knowledge, and executable skills into three distinct layers. When an agent explores tasks, it continuously compiles lessons into a structured knowledge base instead of updating code prompts directly. Subsequent skill synthesis builds on top of this consolidated wiki.\n\nThe empirical results show an unexpected scaling dynamic: smaller language models equipped with evolved skills frequently outperform much larger models that lack systematic skill synthesis. Furthermore, skills evolved by one model architecture transfer cleanly to completely different foundation models without retraining.\n\nSeparating persistent operational memory from execution logic turns out to be the real bottleneck in building reliable software agents."
    },
    {
