      "image": null,
      "content": "Running untrusted code from autonomous coding agents inside container sandboxes is risky because standard containers share the host kernel. Giving each agent invocation its own ephemeral microVM solves this, but running Firecracker on developer workstations usually requires root and bare metal Linux.\n\nThis architecture successfully runs unprivileged Firecracker microVMs nested inside WSL2 on Windows with sub-second launch times. A clean microVM boots in under 0.2 seconds, and a full coding agent environment takes about one second.\n\nThe isolation model nests bubblewrap inside a systemd user scope to restrict cgroups, drops all capabilities, mounts a read-only root, and retains only kvm group access. The guest microVM has no external network interface; all host-guest traffic multiplexes through an isolated vsock broker that proxies traffic strictly to designated hosts.\n\nDefense-in-depth for AI agents requires hardware-level virtualization, and you do not need bare-metal servers or root daemon privileges to achieve it."
    },
    {
