      "image": null,
      "content": "Running Kubernetes workloads directly against Apple Silicon hardware usually hits a wall because tools like KubeVirt depend strictly on Linux KVM. Because macOS lacks KVM, engineering teams running local developer clusters on Mac workstations cannot schedule virtual machine primitives or natively tap into the host unified memory.\n\nKubeSilicon works around this constraint by substituting KVM with the native Apple Virtualization framework. By deploying a local daemon on macOS that watches custom Kubernetes resources, it can orchestrate both lightweight Linux virtual machines and native GPU workloads without leaving your standard declarative workflow.\n\nFor machine learning workflows, this allows Kubeflow pipelines running in local development environments to dispatch tasks directly to Apple unified memory through PyTorch MPS or MLX. Instead of spinning up expensive remote cloud instances for small training verification loops, developers can bridge the gap using standard container scheduling patterns.\n\nBridging disparate operating system virtualization primitives into unified orchestration APIs provides a practical blueprint for hybrid developer environments."
    },
    {
      "title": "Extracting off-heap JSON fields eliminates Java garbage collection pauses",
      "source": "github",
      "url": "https://github.com/RedRiotTank/NativeJsonNavigator",
      "date": "2026-11-10",
      "authors": [
        "RedRiotTank"
      ],
      "why_read": "Learn how to query large JSON datasets off-heap using Java 22's Foreign Function and Memory API. This approach avoids building complete in-memory object graphs, virtually eliminating garbage collection pauses and heap allocation overhead.",
      "hn_id": "50439910",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50439910",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Standard Java JSON libraries like Jackson and Gson parse documents into extensive in-memory object graphs. When handling multi-gigabyte files or high-throughput event streams, allocating millions of small node objects causes brutal heap churn and unpredictable Stop-The-World garbage collection pauses.\n\nNative JSON Navigator bypasses JVM heap allocation entirely by taking advantage of the Foreign Function and Memory API introduced in Java 22. Instead of hydrating trees of Java objects, the engine uses memory-mapped files to query fields directly out of off-heap virtual memory managed by the operating system page cache.\n\nBenchmarks show heap allocation dropping to between 290 bytes and 1.7 kilobytes when querying payloads ranging from 10 megabytes to over 100 megabytes. This eliminates tens to hundreds of megabytes of garbage collection overhead per request while preserving fast scalar lookup times.\n\nDesigning infrastructure around memory-mapped off-heap buffers is one of the most effective strategies for removing garbage collector bottlenecks in data-intensive backend systems."
    },
    {
      "title": "Enabling standalone Kendryte K230 neural network compilation from source",
