      "content": "Getting custom machine learning models to run on specialized edge neural processing units often means fighting closed or undocumented vendor toolchains. The Kendryte K230 pairs dual 64-bit RISC-V cores with a hardware neural processing unit, but practical deployment has long been hampered by incomplete upstream tooling.\n\nThis project reverse engineers the compilation target and execution stack for the K230 inside the nncase compiler framework. The implementation provides a fully open-source build path that includes the compiler plugin, a hardware simulator, and the board-level runtime.\n\nA key architectural aspect of this compiler work is how it handles quantized ONNX operators. By directly supporting pre-quantized 8-bit graphs containing QuantizeLinear and DequantizeLinear operator pairs, developers can compile deployable kmodel artifacts without needing to run calibration datasets through vendor-specific closed routines.\n\nDeep compiler tooling that maps directly to silicon instruction sets is essential for unlocking true portability across specialized edge hardware."
    },
    {
      "title": "Three major MCP servers suffer from silent lost updates",
