      "content": "Evaluating AI chips purely on theoretical FLOP per second metrics fundamentally misrepresents real-world inference throughput. A new study shows the NVIDIA GB300 delivers roughly 25 times the inference capacity of an H100 for agentic workloads, despite offering only 2.5 times the raw 8-bit compute speed.\n\nThe discrepancy comes down to operational bottlenecks. Agentic architectures run deep multi-turn sessions with large KV caches and small batch sizes, shifting the critical path from matrix arithmetic to memory bandwidth and inter-chip communication fabric. When requests require rapid token streaming, chips spend more time moving state across interconnects than executing floating-point math.\n\nSystem interconnect topology amplifies this gap significantly. A GB300 NVL72 rack configuration provides nearly double the inference capacity per GPU compared to an eight-way HGX cluster using identical Blackwell silicon, solely because of its seventy-two-chip coherent fabric.\n\nSystem architects sizing inference clusters must stop budgeting for arithmetic compute and begin optimizing for bandwidth and network fabric topology."
